{"id":"rus-t0-0001","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"Здравствуй.","text_romanized":"ZDRAHST-vooy","text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0002","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"Доброе утро.","text_romanized":"DOH-bruh-yeh OO-truh","text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"Good morning.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0003","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"день","text_romanized":"dyehn’","text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"afternoon","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0004","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"Добрый вечер.","text_romanized":"DOH-bryh VYEH-chuhr","text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"Good evening.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0005","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Как дела? [Kak dela?]","text_romanized":"kahg dee-LAH?","text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0006","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"Хорошо, спасибо. [Khorosho, spasiiba]","text_romanized":"khah-rah-SHOH spah-SEE-buh","text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0007","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Очень приятно. [Ochen' Priyatno]","text_romanized":"OH-cheen' pree-YAHT-nuh","text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0008","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"Как Вас зовут? [Kak vas zovut?]","text_romanized":"kahk vahs zah-VOOT?","text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What is your name?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0009","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.please","domain":1,"text_native":"Пожалуйста.","text_romanized":"pah-ZHAH-luh-stuh","text_english":"Please.","source_caption":"Please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Not a filler in English, and a grammatical particle in some of these languages — one word that changes a sentence from an order to a request.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0010","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Спасибо.","text_romanized":"spuh-SEE-buh","text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0011","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"Не за что. [Nye za chto] (Literally \"It's nothing\", can use \"Пожалуйста\" again)","text_romanized":"NYEH-zuh-shtoh","text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0012","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"Извините.","text_romanized":"eez-vee-NEET-yeh","text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me. (getting attention)","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0013","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.sorry","domain":1,"text_native":"Простите.","text_romanized":"prah-STEET-yeh","text_english":"I am sorry.","source_caption":"I'm sorry.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Apologising for a small fault — bumping someone, being late, not understanding. The form you use signals how much of a fault you think it was.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0014","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"До свидания. [Do svidaniya]","text_romanized":"duh svee-DAH-nyah","text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0015","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"Я не понимаю.","text_romanized":"yah nee puh-nee-MIGH-yoo","text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0016","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-know","domain":12,"text_native":"Я не знаю.","text_romanized":"yah nee ZNAH-yoo","text_english":"I do not know.","source_caption":"I don't know.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The honest answer, and in some cultures the only acceptable one — guessing when you don't know reads as deceit rather than as effort.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"rus-t0-0017","lang":"rus","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"Вы говорите по-английски? [Vyih govoritye po-angliiski?]","text_romanized":"vyh guh-vah-REE-tyeh pah ahn-GLEES-kee?","text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Russian phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Russian_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
